WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.200
Last time we explored water at rest.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.160
Today, let's use pressure to lift a load, then follow a fluid as it moves.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.400
Picture a hydraulic lift with a small piston connected by liquid to a larger

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:14.700
piston.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.560
Push on the small piston, and you increase the pressure of the enclosed fluid.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.540
Pascal's principle says that applied pressure change is transmitted throughout

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:25.040
the fluid.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.920
Pressure is shared; force depends on piston area.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:33.860
For ideal pistons at the same height, with negligible friction and piston

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:39.060
weight, input force divided by input area equals output force divided by output

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.380
area.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.840
Suppose the output piston has ten times the area of the input piston.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.660
Push with fifty newtons.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.680
What load force can it balance?

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:48.920
Take a moment.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:51.700
Ten times fifty gives five hundred newtons.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.760
That feels like a useful bargain.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.320
But a bigger force doesn't mean we've created energy.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.700
We'll check the distance moved next.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.600
If the pistons are at different heights, include the hydrostatic pressure

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.040
difference.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.680
Pascal's principle concerns the transmitted pressure change; it doesn't erase

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.000
the existing variation of pressure with depth.

00:01:11.256 --> 00:01:13.736
Let's see where the bargain comes from.

00:01:14.556 --> 00:01:18.216
Our liquid is approximately incompressible, and we're neglecting leaks.

00:01:19.056 --> 00:01:22.816
When the small piston moves down, the volume it pushes into the connecting

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:26.396
region must equal the volume raised beneath the large piston.

00:01:27.696 --> 00:01:29.876
Piston volume is area times distance moved.

00:01:30.676 --> 00:01:35.856
So input area times input displacement equals output area times output

00:01:35.856 --> 00:01:36.316
displacement.

00:01:37.016 --> 00:01:41.896
If the large area is ten times the small area, the large piston travels only

00:01:41.896 --> 00:01:43.056
one tenth as far.

00:01:43.996 --> 00:01:48.096
Push the small piston down twenty centimetres, and the large piston rises two

00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:49.196
centimetres.

00:01:49.196 --> 00:01:49.956
Now check the work.

00:01:50.696 --> 00:01:54.036
Fifty newtons times zero point two zero metres is ten joules.

00:01:54.036 --> 00:01:58.716
Five hundred newtons times zero point zero two metres is also ten joules.

00:01:59.636 --> 00:02:02.876
Larger force, smaller distance: energy is accounted for.

00:02:03.376 --> 00:02:06.956
Real machines lose some energy through friction and other effects, so their

00:02:06.956 --> 00:02:08.396
useful output work is smaller.

00:02:09.276 --> 00:02:10.876
Here is a JEE trap to dodge.

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:15.516
If a question doubles piston radius, its area becomes four times larger.

00:02:16.496 --> 00:02:20.296
Force multiplication depends on the area ratio, not directly on the radius

00:02:20.296 --> 00:02:20.696
ratio.

00:02:21.556 --> 00:02:23.896
Start with pi r squared before making the comparison.

00:02:24.216 --> 00:02:28.176
Now open a flow path and watch liquid move through a pipe.

00:02:29.296 --> 00:02:30.776
Imagine marking two cross sections.

00:02:31.596 --> 00:02:34.756
In steady flow, fluid doesn't keep accumulating between them.

00:02:35.636 --> 00:02:38.956
The mass entering each second equals the mass leaving each second.

00:02:39.476 --> 00:02:43.276
For constant density, the volume flow rate is the same at both sections.

00:02:43.976 --> 00:02:48.116
We call it capital Q, and calculate it as area times mean flow speed.

00:02:48.736 --> 00:02:53.896
This gives the continuity equation: area one times speed one equals area two

00:02:53.896 --> 00:02:55.036
times speed two.

00:02:55.536 --> 00:02:59.196
Suppose a pipe section has area four square centimetres and mean speed two

00:02:59.196 --> 00:02:59.916
metres per second.

00:03:00.756 --> 00:03:05.476
Convert the area: four square centimetres is zero point zero zero zero four

00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:06.036
square metres.

00:03:07.016 --> 00:03:11.496
The volume flow rate is zero point zero zero zero eight cubic metres per

00:03:11.496 --> 00:03:14.316
second, or zero point eight litres per second.

00:03:14.996 --> 00:03:19.096
At a section of area one square centimetre, the speed becomes eight metres per

00:03:19.096 --> 00:03:19.276
second.

00:03:19.836 --> 00:03:23.176
The liquid speeds up to pass the same volume through a smaller opening.

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:28.316
We're assuming steady incompressible flow, a full pipe, and no leaks or

00:03:28.316 --> 00:03:29.536
branches between these sections.

00:03:30.336 --> 00:03:34.396
For changing density, conserve mass using density times area times speed

00:03:34.396 --> 00:03:34.896
instead.

00:03:35.816 --> 00:03:39.176
Continuity comes from conservation of mass, not from a pressure equation.

00:03:39.432 --> 00:03:42.072
Continuity tells us how flow speed changes.

00:03:42.932 --> 00:03:45.712
Bernoulli's equation connects that speed to pressure and height.

00:03:46.472 --> 00:03:50.892
For steady incompressible flow with negligible viscous losses, along a

00:03:50.892 --> 00:03:55.872
streamline, add three terms: pressure, one half density times speed squared,

00:03:56.272 --> 00:03:58.132
and density times g times height.

00:03:58.672 --> 00:04:03.672
Their sum stays constant when no pump or turbine adds or removes energy between

00:04:03.672 --> 00:04:04.172
the points.

00:04:04.952 --> 00:04:07.652
Think of this as an energy account per unit volume.

00:04:08.412 --> 00:04:12.352
Now take two points at the same height in an ideal water flow.

00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:15.572
Speed increases from two to four metres per second.

00:04:16.292 --> 00:04:17.552
How much does pressure decrease?

00:04:18.232 --> 00:04:19.232
The height terms cancel.

00:04:19.972 --> 00:04:23.752
The pressure drop is one half times one thousand times the difference between

00:04:23.752 --> 00:04:25.112
four squared and two squared.

00:04:25.112 --> 00:04:29.732
Sixteen minus four is twelve, giving six thousand pascals.

00:04:30.392 --> 00:04:35.007
If the first pressure is one hundred twenty thousand pascals, the second is one

00:04:35.007 --> 00:04:36.372
hundred fourteen thousand.

00:04:36.372 --> 00:04:39.592
Faster flow has lower pressure in this particular comparison.

00:04:40.492 --> 00:04:44.252
Keep the conditions attached to that conclusion: equal height, the same

00:04:44.252 --> 00:04:46.092
streamline, and the ideal assumptions.

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:49.772
Don't automatically apply that slogan to every moving fluid.

00:04:50.512 --> 00:04:54.552
First decide whether continuity, energy conservation, or both are needed.

00:04:54.696 --> 00:04:57.316
Let's finish with a tank draining through a small opening.

00:04:57.996 --> 00:05:01.476
The tank is open to the atmosphere, and the outlet is also open to air.

00:05:02.356 --> 00:05:05.656
Choose one point at the free surface and the other in the emerging jet.

00:05:06.276 --> 00:05:09.856
Their pressures are both atmospheric, so those terms cancel in Bernoulli's

00:05:09.856 --> 00:05:10.196
equation.

00:05:10.936 --> 00:05:15.016
If the tank is much wider than the outlet, continuity tells us the surface

00:05:15.016 --> 00:05:17.796
moves down much more slowly than the jet moves out.

00:05:18.616 --> 00:05:19.396
Neglect the surface speed.

00:05:20.016 --> 00:05:24.856
A vertical drop h then becomes kinetic energy per unit volume, giving outlet

00:05:24.856 --> 00:05:27.976
speed equal to the square root of two g h.

00:05:27.976 --> 00:05:33.236
With a depth of one point two five metres and g equal to ten, two g h is twenty

00:05:33.236 --> 00:05:33.736
five.

00:05:33.736 --> 00:05:36.336
The ideal exit speed is five metres per second.

00:05:37.196 --> 00:05:40.156
Pause and predict what happens when the depth becomes four times larger.

00:05:40.736 --> 00:05:42.976
The speed doubles, because of the square root.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:47.856
Real outlet flow can differ because of viscosity, jet contraction and other

00:05:47.856 --> 00:05:48.236
losses.

00:05:48.476 --> 00:05:53.276
As a large tank drains, we use this as a slowly changing, approximately steady

00:05:53.276 --> 00:05:54.676
model at each instant.

00:05:55.316 --> 00:05:59.496
Your checklist is pressure reference, vertical height, flow continuity and

00:05:59.496 --> 00:06:00.136
energy assumptions.

00:06:01.036 --> 00:06:04.396
Next we'll need separate ideas for viscosity and surface tension.
